 @DeadNoob451  or maybe some of us genuinely like it? It’s a very important feature for the future of gaming. No one can deny that. It’s just not at all widely there yet is all. Shadows back in the day is in the exact same shoes as global illuminations and ray tracing right now. 

I’ve been only buying bang for buck GPUs like rx580 into a RX5700xt and now a RTX 3060ti. But For two years I’ve been dying to play ray tracing on. It’s such a massive difference idk why people discredit it. Games like Metro Exodus look much more stunning when combined with rasterization than rasterization alone

I’ve been waiting for a good value ray tracing like a 3060ti for two years now. And if I was going to spend $1000 on a GPU, of course I’m going to play with ray tracing and expect good performance. It’s no excuse for either company to not be there at this tier of pricing even with our current tech.

 @ZDY66666  Its not at all important, and this "omg its the future" nonsense comes directly from Nvidias marketing fluff. Same company that made folks like you believe that hairworks was "the future" a few years back. The only reason why some games look much worse without RTX is that the developers just put no effort into the conventional graphics if Nvidia is paying them to make RTX look as good as possible. 
Its just buzzword filled marketing to hook the people that always need the newest vaporware even if they dont understand what it does or how it works, or else they are not happy. And that tactic has worked perfectly for decades and mislead millions of consumers.
But hey, dont let me stop you from enjoying your 2020 remake of blast processing. Its totally the future. A company would never lie to you about such things after all.

